<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Crimean Regional Government</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Simferopol</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Two successive governments of the Crimea in 1918 and 1919, first under German protection and then under Allied and White support.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After German troops occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in spring 1918 and ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taurida-soviet-socialist-republic">Taurida Soviet Socialist Republic</a>, they backed a regional government led by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maciej-sulkiewicz">Maciej Sulkiewicz</a>, a Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> former Russian officer, formed in June 1918.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maciej-sulkiewicz">Sulkiewicz</a>'s government claimed autonomy and refused to accept rule by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ukrainian-state">Ukrainian State</a>, which blockaded the peninsula. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> withdrew in November 1918, a liberal second government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-krym">Solomon Krym</a>, a Karaite agronomist, took over, relying on Allied warships and the White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a>. Both governments had little control over their own security or economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In April 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> broke into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> evacuated and the Krym government left by sea. The Bolsheviks then set up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crimean-socialist-soviet-republic">Crimean Socialist Soviet Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/crimean-regional-government.jpg" alt="Crimean Regional Government" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Crimean_Regional_Government.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Simferopol</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maciej Sulkiewicz, Solomon Krym</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian, Crimean Tatar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crimean Regional Government (1918-1919)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=crimean-regional-government" aria-label="Crimean Regional Government on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5492 590 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Crimean Regional Government lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5812 846L5300 622M5812 846L5812 846"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5812 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5812 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5812 846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Simferopol. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=crimean-regional-government">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

